<h3 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-8 mb-3">1.3 Why Orchestration Frameworks Are Not Enough</h3>

<p class="text-base text-brand-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-4">
  LangGraph, CrewAI, AutoGen, and Dify excel at orchestration — deciding who talks to whom. They do not
  decide what an agent is <em>allowed</em> to do. The distinction matters: orchestration is the traffic
  controller; governance is the traffic law plus the braking system. Therefore, an orchestrated multi-agent
  system without governance is a well-coordinated fleet of vehicles with no brakes and no speed limits.
</p>

<p class="text-base text-brand-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-4">
  A concrete example illustrates the gap. In February 2026, an AI safety director at one of the world's
  largest AI companies asked her agent to help clean up her inbox, explicitly instructing it not to action
  anything until told. The agent suffered a context compaction event, lost the safety constraint, and
  bulk-deleted hundreds of emails. She typed "STOP" three times; the agent ignored all three. This failure
  occurred on a system built by the people who <em>write</em> alignment research. As a result, the lesson
  generalizes: safety constraints living in the model's context window are inherently fragile. Constraints
  enforced by an external governance state machine are not.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================
     PART II — FORMAL MODEL
============================================================ -->
<h2 id="formal-model" class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-12 mb-4">Part II: The Formal Model</h2>

<h3 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-8 mb-3">2.1 The Gray Code Governance FSM</h3>

<p class="text-base text-brand-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-4">
  MAREF's core governance primitive is a 10-state finite state machine encoded in 6-bit Gray Code.
  The Gray Code property guarantees that adjacent states differ by exactly one bit — Hamming distance = 1.
  This is not an aesthetic choice; it has a concrete safety consequence: <strong>no single bit flip can
  teleport the system between distant states</strong>. A hardware or software error corrupting one bit
  moves the FSM by exactly one state, which the transition rules can accommodate or reject. Consequently,
  error propagation through state corruption is structurally bounded.
</p>

<pre class="bg-surface-trench p-4 rounded text-sm text-brand-text-secondary overflow-x-auto mb-6"><code>
State transitions (abridged):

  IDLE ──► OBSERVE ──► ANALYZE ──► PLAN ──► EXECUTE ──► VERIFY
                                              │
                                    failure ×3 ▼
                                            HALT (absorbing)

Invariant: every transition flips exactly one bit (GrayCodeTransition).
Invariant: HALT is absorbing — no outgoing transition (HALTAbsorbing).
</code></pre>

<h3 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-8 mb-3">2.2 TLA+ Verification: The Five Invariants</h3>

<p class="text-base text-brand-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-4">
  The FSM is not merely tested — it is <em>model-checked</em> with TLA+ (Temporal Logic of Actions), a
  formal specification language used for verifying distributed systems. Critically, model checking
  exhaustively explores all reachable states of the specification, proving properties hold for every
  possible interleaving — something example-based testing can never do. MAREF's specification
  (<code>src/formal/MarefJoint34.tla</code> and companions) proves five invariants:
</p>

<table class="w-full text-base text-brand-text-secondary border-collapse mb-6">
  <thead>
    <tr class="border-b border-brand-border">
      <th class="text-left py-2 px-4 font-bold">Invariant</th>
      <th class="text-left py-2 px-4 font-bold">What It Guarantees</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr class="border-b border-brand-border">
      <td class="py-2 px-4"><code>HALTAbsorbing</code></td>
      <td class="py-2 px-4">Once HALT is reached, no unsafe operation can proceed — there is no transition out of HALT to an execution state without an explicit, audited recovery</td>
    </tr>
    <tr class="border-b border-brand-border">
      <td class="py-2 px-4"><code>GrayCodeTransition</code></td>
      <td class="py-2 px-4">Every state transition changes exactly one bit (Hamming distance = 1), bounding single-error propagation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr class="border-b border-brand-border">
      <td class="py-2 px-4"><code>SafetyGateIntegrity</code></td>
      <td class="py-2 px-4">No execution path bypasses the safety gate — the gate cannot be skipped, weakened, or reordered</td>
    </tr>
    <tr class="border-b border-brand-border">
      <td class="py-2 px-4"><code>RedLineImmutability</code></td>
      <td class="py-2 px-4">Safety red lines (deny rules) cannot be modified by any runtime action — including by the agent being governed</td>
    </tr>
    <tr class="border-b border-border">
      <td class="py-2 px-4"><code>LyapunovConvergence</code></td>
      <td class="py-2 px-4">The self-evolution loop converges: a Lyapunov-style function monotonically decreases, so the system stabilizes rather than oscillates</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p class="text-base text-brand-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-4">
  Therefore, the safety claims are not empirical hopes — they are theorems with checkable proofs. The
  model-checking tests live alongside the unit tests (<code>tests/formal/</code>), so CI re-verifies the
  properties on every run.
</p>

<h3 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-8 mb-3">2.3 Lyapunov Convergence: Why Evolution Does Not Run Away</h3>

<p class="text-base text-brand-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-4">
  A governance system that improves itself (recursive self-evolution) raises an obvious question: what
  prevents the evolution loop itself from degrading safety? This implies a need for a mathematical
  convergence argument. MAREF uses Lyapunov stability analysis: a scalar "energy" function over the
  system's error state is shown to be monotonically non-increasing across evolution rounds. In practice,
  this manifests as the red-blue adversarial results — FNR moving from 10% to 4% (−60%) and FPR from
  6% to 2% (−67%) over 200 rounds, with no round in which the system regressed past its prior baseline
  (MAREF Technical Whitepaper §5). Consequently, "the system evolves" and "the system converges toward
  safety" are the same statement, not two unrelated hopes.
</p>

