<h2 id="deployment" class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-12 mb-4">Part VI: Production Deployment</h2>

<h3 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-8 mb-3">6.1 Adoption Sequence</h3>

<ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-base text-brand-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-4 space-y-3">
  <li><strong>Install.</strong> <code>pip install maref</code> (full) or <code>pip install maref-lite</code> (zero-config overlay).</li>
  <li><strong>Attach.</strong> Wrap your orchestrator with <code>GovernanceOverlay</code> — no changes to agent logic.</li>
  <li><strong>Route tool calls.</strong> Every tool invocation passes through the permission check and policy decision tree before execution.</li>
  <li><strong>Enable audit.</strong> Turn on the Merkle audit chain; export to your SIEM via OpenTelemetry.</li>
  <li><strong>Observe.</strong> Run <code>maref status</code>; watch the Trust Engine drift metrics and circuit breaker counters.</li>
  <li><strong>Prove.</strong> Run the reproducible benchmarks and attach the output to your compliance evidence package.</li>
</ol>

<h3 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-8 mb-3">6.2 What Governance Does NOT Do</h3>

<p class="text-base text-brand-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-4">
  Intellectual honesty requires stating boundaries:
</p>

<ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-base text-brand-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-4 space-y-2">
  <li>MAREF does not replace your orchestrator — it is the layer below orchestration.</li>
  <li>It does not make a weak model strong — it makes a capable model accountable.</li>
  <li>The ~11 ms overhead is host-dependent; reproduce it on your own hardware before citing it in an SLA.</li>
  <li>Human-in-the-loop escalation (~3% of decisions) means someone must actually be on call.</li>
  <li>Lyapunov convergence is a property of the specified evolution loop under the stated assumptions — it is not a blanket guarantee against adversarial inputs outside the threat model.</li>
</ul>

<h3 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-8 mb-3">6.3 The Decision Framework</h3>

<p class="text-base text-brand-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-4">
  Therefore, the adoption question reduces to a simple test: <strong>can your current agent stack produce
  a cryptographically signed record proving that a specific tool call was authorized by a specific policy
  at a specific time, and can it halt execution within one state transition when that policy is violated?</strong>
  If the answer is no, you have orchestration without governance — and the evidence above shows where that
  trajectory ends.
</p>

